PVS (Pearson VPN Service)
=========================

Automated OpenVPN connection rotator, monitoring, and management service.

Supports ExpressVPN and ProtonVPN configurations.


Installation
------------

    sudo apt-get install -f ./pvs_<version>_amd64.deb


Setup
-----

1. Add VPN Configs

   Place your .ovpn config files in one of the following directories:

     /usr/bin/pvs/protonvpn/vpn_configs/
     /usr/bin/pvs/expressvpn/vpn_configs/

   Download these from your VPN provider's website. Only .ovpn files are
   accepted. Set openvpn.config_dir in config.yml to point to the directory
   you are using.

2. Set VPN Credentials

   Edit the credentials file with your VPN provider's username on line 1
   and password on line 2:

     sudo vi /config/openvpn-credentials.txt

   Ensure correct permissions:

     sudo chmod 600 /config/openvpn-credentials.txt

3. Edit Configuration

   The main configuration file is at:

     /usr/bin/pvs/config.yml

   Key settings:

     openvpn.config_dir
       Path to the directory containing your .ovpn files.
       Default: vpn_configs/ (relative to /usr/bin/pvs/)
       Example: /usr/bin/pvs/protonvpn/vpn_configs

     openvpn.preferred_configs
       Optional list of specific .ovpn filenames to rotate through.
       If empty, PVS selects randomly from all .ovpn files in config_dir.

     openvpn.rotate_minutes
       How often to rotate to a new VPN server (default: 15).

     openvpn.network_check_minutes
       How often to perform DNS health checks (default: 1).
       Should be less than rotate_minutes.

     firewall.enabled
       WARNING: Set to false if your server has only one network interface,
       otherwise you will be locked out! The firewall blocks all non-VPN
       traffic on the specified interface when the VPN is down.

     firewall.privateNetworkSubnet
       Your LAN subnet (e.g., 192.168.1.0/24).

     firewall.privateNetworkInterface
       Your LAN-facing network interface name (e.g., enp2s0).

     api.bind_address
       Address for the HTTP status API (default: 127.0.0.1:8080).

4. Enable and Start

     sudo systemctl daemon-reload
     sudo systemctl enable pvs.service
     sudo systemctl start pvs.service

5. Verify

     sudo systemctl status pvs.service
     tail -f /var/log/pvs.log


File Locations
--------------

  Binary:         /usr/bin/pvs/pvs
  Configuration:  /usr/bin/pvs/config.yml
  Credentials:    /config/openvpn-credentials.txt
  Systemd unit:   /etc/systemd/system/pvs.service
  Log file:       /var/log/pvs.log
  VPN configs:    /usr/bin/pvs/<provider>/vpn_configs/*.ovpn


Troubleshooting
---------------

  Check service status:
    sudo systemctl status pvs.service

  View live logs:
    tail -f /var/log/pvs.log

  View systemd journal:
    sudo journalctl -u pvs.service -f

  Find failing VPN hosts:
    cat /var/log/pvs.log | grep "Cannot resolve host address:" | \
      sed 's/.*Cannot resolve host address: \([^ ]*\).*/\1/' | \
      sort | uniq -c | sort -nr

  If the VPN won't connect, check that:
    - .ovpn files exist in the configured config_dir
    - Credentials file has correct username/password
    - resolvconf is installed (required dependency)
    - The VPN provider still supports the server endpoints in your configs
